<h2>The allegation</h2>
<p>On 3 June 2025, the complainant was confronted and assaulted outside a wine shop in E-Block, Nand Nagri. The prosecution&rsquo;s case was that the applicant held the complainant while two co-accused assaulted him, one of them using a knife. The applicant was arrested the next day and remained in judicial custody. His defence emphasised that he caused no injury himself and that the knife was recovered at the co-accused&rsquo;s instance, not his.</p>

<h2>The procedural picture at the time of hearing</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>June 2025</strong> — FIR registered; applicant in custody from 4 June 2025.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Investigation</strong> — completed; chargesheet filed.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>9 April 2026</strong> — charges framed under Sections 109(1) and 3(5) BNS.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>October 2026</strong> — prosecution evidence yet to begin in earnest; the complainant, a material witness, not examined, with bailable warrants issued after he failed to appear.</div>
</div>

<h2>The reasoning</h2>
<p>Justice Sachin Datta granted bail on a convergence of familiar but carefully applied factors. Continued detention would serve no further purpose once the investigation was complete. The applicant&rsquo;s role was distinct from that of the co-accused who wielded the weapon — a point that matters both to the gravity assessment and to eventual sentencing exposure. Prior involvements that had not ended in conviction could not by themselves justify refusal, on the authority of <em>Prabhakar Tewari v. State of U.P.</em> And the pace of the trial — charges framed ten months into custody, the star witness still unexamined — engaged the settled principle that Article 21&rsquo;s speedy-trial guarantee applies irrespective of the seriousness of the offence, for which the Court drew on <em>Kapil Wadhawan v. CBI</em> and <em>Arvind Dham v. Enforcement Directorate</em>, warning against converting pretrial detention into a form of punishment.</p>

<div class="compare">
<div class="col old"><h3>Prosecution framing</h3><p>An attempt-to-murder case with a weapon, prior involvements, and a shared common intention under Section 3(5) BNS.</p></div>
<div class="arrow">→</div>
<div class="col new"><h3>Bail court framing</h3><p>A role-specific inquiry: who held, who struck, what was recovered from whom, how long custody has run, and when the trial will realistically conclude.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Why the case is useful</h2>
<p>Role attribution is among the most reliable levers in bail practice for multi-accused assault cases. Common intention under Section 3(5) BNS may keep an accused in the array for trial, but at the bail stage courts distinguish between the hand that wielded the weapon and the hand that held the victim. Combined with completed investigation and demonstrable trial delay not attributable to the accused, that distinction will frequently carry a regular bail application — on conditions robust enough to protect the witnesses who are yet to depose.</p>
<div class="note">
<p>The order is also a reminder of how systematically Delhi High Court bail orders now address witness protection: no contact with the complainant or witnesses, location-enabled mobile reporting, and monthly attendance at the police station are standard conditions, and breach invites cancellation under Section 483(3) BNSS.</p>
</div>
